==Icelandic==

===Etymology===
From {{der|is|non|herra}}, from {{der|is|osx|hērro}}, from {{der|is|odt|hēriro}}, {{der|is|goh|hērro}}, the comparative form of {{m|goh|hēr||noble, venerable}} ({{cog|de|hehr}}), by analogy with Latin {{m|la|senior||elder}}. Cognates include Danish, Norwegian and {{cog|sv|herre}}, {{cog|nl|heer}}, {{cog|de|Herr}}. The Old High German word originally meant “grey, grey-haired”, and descends from {{der|is|gem-pro|*hairaz||grey}}, making it cognate with {{cog|ang|hār}} ({{cog|en|hoar}}), {{cog|non|hárr}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|is|/ˈhɛrːa/}}
*: {{rhymes|is|ɛrːa}}

===Noun===
{{is-noun|m|herra|herrar}}

# [[lord]], [[master]]
# the [[Lord]] ([[God]])
# an honorific title for a [[bishop]]; [[Lord]]
# an honorific title for a [[noble]]; [[Lord]]
# an honorific title for the highest state officials, now especially the [[president]], but also a {{m|is|sýslumaður}} (the local official of the state in each {{m|is|sýsla}}), [[ambassador]], etc.; [[Excellency]]
# [[mister]] (general honorific for a man, especially in writing; usually abbreviated: {{m|is|hr.}})
# a general deferential form of address to a male; [[sir]]
# [[gentleman]] {{gloss|general polite term for a male}}
#: {{m|is|dömur mínar og herrar}}
#:: Ladies and '''gentlemen'''.

====Usage notes====
Used as an honorific title (prenominally or on its own) for bishops and for the president of Iceland (also, especially historically, for some other dignitaries). Also used as a general courtesy title for men, equivalent to English {{m|en|mister}}, but then primarily in formal written correspondence and abbreviated {{m|is|hr.}} However, when it is used as an honorific title for bishops, presidents, etc., it should never be abbreviated. The equivalent female title is {{m|is|frú}} (in both contexts).

====Declension====
{{is-decl-noun-m-w1a|h|e|rr}}

====Derived terms====
* {{l|is|andlangs herra}}
* {{l|is|dömur mínar og herrar}}
* {{l|is|herramaður}}

====See also====
* {{lb|is|archaic}} {{l|is|herri}}

